= Solution
{parent=b}
{c}

Write $u=(a,b)^T$, $v=(c,d)^T$ and $\Delta=ad-bc$. If $xu+yv=0$, then
$$
ax+cy=0,\qquad bx+dy=0.
$$
Eliminating $y$ gives $\Delta x=0$, and eliminating $x$ gives $\Delta y=0$. Hence $\Delta\ne0$ implies <linear independence>.

For the converse, suppose $\Delta=0$. If $u=0$, the family already contains the <zero vector> and is <linearly dependent>. If $a\ne0$, the equality $ad=bc$ gives $v=(c/a)u$. If $a=0$ but $u\ne0$, then $b\ne0$ and $bc=0$ forces $c=0$, so $v=(d/b)u$. Both cases give a nontrivial <linear relation>. Therefore
$$
\boxed{u,v\text{ are linearly independent}\iff ad-bc\ne0.}
$$
This is the two-dimensional <determinant> criterion, proved directly without assuming that criterion.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

Subtracting the first <vector> from each of the other two preserves the <basis> property by <1/a/iii>. The resulting family is
$$
e_1,\quad(0,\alpha,\alpha^2)^T,\quad(0,\beta,\beta^2)^T.
$$
Its last two <vectors> belong to the plane with first coordinate zero. By <1/b>, they are <linearly independent> there precisely when
$$
\alpha\beta^2-\beta\alpha^2=\alpha\beta(\beta-\alpha)\ne0.
$$
In that case their two-dimensional <span> is the whole plane, and adjoining $e_1$ gives a <basis> of $\mathbb R^3$. If the displayed expression vanishes, a nontrivial <linear relation> among those two <vectors> prevents a <basis>. Thus
$$
\boxed{\alpha\ne0,\quad\beta\ne0,\quad\alpha\ne\beta.}
$$

