By definition, the <image of a linear map> $T_A$ is
$$
\operatorname{im}T_A=\{A\mathbf x:\mathbf x\in\mathbb R^m\}.
$$
Consequently $\mathbf b\in\operatorname{im}T_A$ means exactly that some $\mathbf x\in\mathbb R^m$ satisfies $A\mathbf x=\mathbf b$. This is precisely the existence of a solution of the <linear system>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Suppose $\mathbf x_0$ is one solution. By <linearity>,
$$
A\mathbf x=\mathbf b
\iff A(\mathbf x-\mathbf x_0)=0
\iff\mathbf x-\mathbf x_0\in\ker T_A.
$$
Thus the <solution set of a linear system> is exactly $\mathbf x_0+\ker T_A$. It has one member if the <kernel> is $\{0\}$. Conversely, if there is a unique solution and $\mathbf z\in\ker T_A$, then $\mathbf x_0+\mathbf z$ is also a solution, forcing $\mathbf z=0$. Combining this with <2/a/i> gives
$$
\boxed{\text{unique solution}\iff\mathbf b\in\operatorname{im}T_A\text{ and }\ker T_A=\{0\}.}
$$

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

If $\mathbf b$ is outside the <image of a linear map> $T_A$, there is no solution. Otherwise choose $\mathbf x_0$ with $A\mathbf x_0=\mathbf b$. By <2/a/ii>, a zero <kernel> gives a unique solution. If the <kernel> contains a nonzero <vector> $\mathbf z$, all
$$
\mathbf x_0+t\mathbf z,\qquad t\in\mathbb R,
$$
are solutions. They are distinct, since $(t-s)\mathbf z=0$ forces $t=s$. Hence there are infinitely many. These alternatives exhaust all possibilities.

