= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

**No.** Here $A$ has more rows than columns, which does not force a nonzero <kernel>. For example, with $m=1$, $n=2$,
$$
A=\begin{pmatrix}1\\0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\mathbf b=\begin{pmatrix}0\\0\end{pmatrix},
$$
the <linear system> has the unique solution $x=0$. With the same $A$ and $\mathbf b=(0,1)^T$ it has no solution at all.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

**No.** Take $A=I_m$ and $\mathbf b=0$. The <homogeneous linear system> then has only the solution $\mathbf x=0$. Homogeneity ensures existence, but infinitely many solutions would additionally require a nonzero <kernel>.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

**No.** The <rank-nullity theorem> gives
$$
\dim\ker T_A=m-\operatorname{rank}T_A\geq m-n>0,
$$
but this guarantees infinitely many solutions only when the <linear system> is consistent. For a counterexample take
$$
n=1,\quad m=2,\quad A=\begin{pmatrix}0&0\end{pmatrix},\quad\mathbf b=(1).
$$
There is no solution, since the left side always vanishes.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}
{c}

**Yes.** A <surjective> $T_A$ has $\operatorname{im}T_A=\mathbb R^n$, so every $\mathbf b$ is attained. Its <rank of a linear map> is $n$, and the <rank-nullity theorem> gives
$$
\dim\ker T_A=m-n>0.
$$
The <solution set of a linear system> is therefore a translate of a nonzero <vector subspace>, containing infinitely many solutions as in <2/a/iii>.

