= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

Since $S'$ is the <restriction> of $S$ to $\operatorname{im}T$,
$$
\operatorname{im}(ST)=\{S(Tu):u\in U\}
=\{S(v):v\in\operatorname{im}T\}=\operatorname{im}S'.
$$
This <image of a linear map> is contained in $\operatorname{im}S$, so $r_{ST}\leq r_S$. Also, applying $S$ to a <basis> of $\operatorname{im}T$ gives a <spanning set> for $\operatorname{im}S'$, of size $r_T$. Thus $r_{ST}\leq r_T$, and
$$
\boxed{r_{ST}\leq\min(r_S,r_T).}
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

For $u\in U$, the <vector> $Tu$ already belongs to $\operatorname{im}T$, so
$$
STu=0\iff S'(Tu)=0\iff Tu\in\ker S'.
$$
Therefore $\ker(ST)=T^{-1}(\ker S')$. Since $\ker S'=\ker S\cap\operatorname{im}T$, its intersection with $\operatorname{im}T$ is itself. Applying <3/a> with $X=\ker S'$ yields
$$
\boxed{n_{ST}=n_{S'}+n_T\leq n_S+n_T.}
$$
The inequality follows from the inclusion $\ker S'\subseteq\ker S$ and monotonicity of <dimension> for <vector subspaces>.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

By <3/b/i> and <3/b/ii>,
$$
r_{ST}+n_{ST}=r_{S'}+n_{S'}+n_T.
$$
The <domain of a function> of $S'$ is $\operatorname{im}T$. Its <rank-nullity theorem> gives $r_{S'}+n_{S'}=\dim\operatorname{im}T=r_T$. Applying the <rank-nullity theorem> for $T$ now gives
$$
\boxed{r_{ST}+n_{ST}=r_T+n_T=\dim U,}
$$
which is exactly the <rank-nullity theorem> for the <function composition> $ST$.

