= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

If $T^2=I$, every $v\in V$ has the decomposition
$$
v=\frac{v+Tv}{2}+\frac{v-Tv}{2},
$$
whose first term belongs to $E_1$ and second to $E_{-1}$. Their intersection is zero by <4/a/ii>, so $V=E_1\oplus E_{-1}$ is a <direct sum>. Choosing <bases> of the two <eigenspaces> gives a diagonal <matrix> with entries $1$ and $-1$.

Since $T^2\ne T$ while $T^2=I$, we have $T\ne I$. Thus $\dim E_{-1}$ is $1$, $2$, or $3$. Reordering the <basis> gives exactly
$$
\boxed{-I_3,\qquad\operatorname{diag}(1,1,-1),\qquad
\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,-1).}
$$
For $-I_3$, $T$ is **point inversion through the origin**. For $\operatorname{diag}(1,1,-1)$, it fixes the plane $E_1$ pointwise and reverses the complementary line $E_{-1}$: if $v=p+\ell$ in this <direct sum>, then $Tv=p-\ell$. For $\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,-1)$, it fixes the line $E_1$ pointwise and reverses the complementary plane $E_{-1}$.

These are instances of <oblique symmetry of complementary subspaces>, allowing oblique <eigenspaces>. They are a Euclidean <reflection in a hyperplane> and a <rotation in three dimensions> through $\pi$ about a line, respectively, when the two <eigenspaces> are perpendicular. The hypotheses do not require an <orthogonal transformation> or an <orthonormal basis>, so that additional metric property cannot be inferred just from the displayed diagonal forms.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

For this <tripotent linear operator>, the relation $T^3=T$ gives $T(T^2-I)=0$. Since $T^2\ne I$, there is $v$ with $(T^2-I)v\ne0$, and that nonzero <vector> belongs to $\ker T$. Hence $n_T\geq1$. Also $T\ne0$, because $T=0$ would imply $T^2=T$. The <rank-nullity theorem> in <dimension> three therefore gives
$$
\boxed{n_T\in\{1,2\}.}
$$
Now suppose $n_T=2$. Then $\operatorname{im}T$ is a line. On this line, $T^2$ is the <identity operator>: if $y=Tx$, then $T^2y=T^3x=Tx=y$. Consequently $T$ acts on this line by a <scalar> $c$ satisfying $c^2=1$. If $c=1$, then $T^2x=Tx$ for every $x$, contrary to $T^2\ne T$. Thus $c=-1$.

Choose a nonzero $w\in\operatorname{im}T$, so $Tw=-w$. It does not belong to $\ker T$. A <basis> $k_1,k_2$ of $\ker T$, followed by $w$, is therefore a <basis> of $V$, in which
$$
\boxed{[T]_{(k_1,k_2,w)}=\begin{pmatrix}0&0&0\\0&0&0\\0&0&-1\end{pmatrix}.}
$$

