= Solution
{parent=a}
{c}

A <transposition> in the <symmetric group> $S_n$ exchanges two distinct symbols and fixes all others. A <permutation cycle> $(a_1\ a_2\ \cdots\ a_k)$ sends $a_j$ to $a_{j+1}$, sends $a_k$ to $a_1$, and fixes the other symbols. Its entries are distinct. For $k=1$ this is the identity <permutation>.

A <group element> has <order of a group element> $k$ if $k$ is the least positive integer for which its $k$th power is the <identity element>. For the displayed <permutation cycle>, its $j$th power sends $a_i$ forward $j$ places, with subscripts read modulo $k$. Hence the $k$th power fixes every symbol, whereas for $0<j<k$ the image of $a_1$ is different from $a_1$. Thus its <order of a group element> is exactly $k$, including $k=1$.

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

For each symbol $a$, iterate the <permutation> $\sigma$. Since the symbol set is finite, some iterate repeats. Applying an inverse power of $\sigma$ shows that the first return is to $a$ itself, giving
$$
a,\sigma(a),\ldots,\sigma^{k-1}(a),\qquad\sigma^k(a)=a,
$$
with the displayed symbols distinct. This is a <permutation cycle>. If two such lists share a symbol, applying powers of $\sigma$ shows that they contain exactly the same symbols. Hence these <group orbits> form a <partition of a set> $\{1,\ldots,n\}$. On each <group orbit>, $\sigma$ acts as the corresponding <permutation cycle>, and these <disjoint permutation cycles> multiply to $\sigma$.

The <disjoint cycle decomposition> is **unique up to reordering the cycles and cyclically rotating the entries within each cycle**, with fixed points optionally omitted. Indeed, each cycle must consist of one of the uniquely determined <group orbits>, and its successive entries must follow the action of $\sigma$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

With products acting from right to left, a <permutation cycle> satisfies
$$
(a_1\ a_2\ \cdots\ a_k)
=(a_1\ a_k)(a_1\ a_{k-1})\cdots(a_1\ a_2).
$$
The right side sends $a_1$ to $a_2$, each $a_j$ to $a_{j+1}$ for $2\leq j<k$, and $a_k$ to $a_1$, while fixing all other symbols. This verifies the formula. Applying it to every cycle in the <disjoint cycle decomposition> expresses any <permutation> as a product of <transpositions>. The identity is represented by the empty product.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

Suppose $\sigma$ has <order of a group element> two. On a cycle of length $k$, $\sigma^2$ moves each entry two places. Since $\sigma^2=e$, every such $k$ divides two, so $k=1$ or $k=2$. After omitting the fixed points, the <disjoint cycle decomposition> is therefore a product of <disjoint permutation cycles> of length two, which are <transpositions>. There is at least one, because $\sigma\ne e$.

= c
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}
{c}

If a <permutation> has disjoint cycle lengths $d_1,\ldots,d_t$, then its $k$th power is the identity exactly when every $d_j$ divides $k$. Thus its <order of a permutation> is $\operatorname{lcm}(d_1,\ldots,d_t)$.

Suppose $n=p^r$ is a <prime power> and $\sigma\in S_n$ has order $n$. Each $d_j$ divides $p^r$, so is a power of $p$. Their <least common multiple> is therefore their maximum, forcing some $d_j=p^r=n$. This cycle already uses all $n$ symbols, so $\sigma$ is an $n$-cycle.

Conversely, if $n>1$ is not a <prime power>, choose a prime-power factor $a=p^r$ containing the full power of one <prime divisor> of $n$, and put $b=n/a$. Then $a,b>1$, they are <coprime>, and $ab=n$. Moreover
$$
ab-a-b=(a-1)(b-1)-1\geq0,
$$
so $a+b\leq n$. We can therefore take disjoint cycles of lengths $a$ and $b$ in $S_n$, fixing all remaining symbols. Their product has <order of a permutation> $\operatorname{lcm}(a,b)=ab=n$, but it is not an $n$-cycle. This proves the <prime-power characterization by full permutation cycles>:
$$
\boxed{\text{every element of order }n\text{ in }S_n\text{ is an }n\text{-cycle}\iff n=p^r.}
$$

= d
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}
{c}

Acting separately on disjoint sets of four and eight symbols embeds the <direct product of groups> $S_4\times S_8$ into $S_{12}$. The action is injective since fixing all twelve symbols forces both factors to be identities.

For the lower bound, $S_4$ contains a cycle of order four and $S_8$ contains disjoint cycles of lengths five and three, whose product has order fifteen. The corresponding element of $S_4\times S_8$ has <order of a group element> $\operatorname{lcm}(4,15)=60$. Every isomorphic subgroup of $S_n$ must therefore contain a <permutation> of order sixty. We can bound the <minimum number of symbols for a permutation of prescribed order> directly here.

In the <disjoint cycle decomposition> of such a <permutation>, all cycle lengths divide $60$, and their <least common multiple> must contain the factors $4$, $3$, and $5$. If these factors occur in three distinct cycles, those cycles use at least $4+3+5=12$ symbols. If two occur in the same cycle, that cycle has length at least their product: combining $4,3$ costs at least $12$, combining $4,5$ costs at least $20$, and combining $3,5$ costs at least $15$. Each possibility already requires at least twelve symbols. Thus $n\geq12$, and
$$
\boxed{n_{\min}=12.}
$$

