= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

A map $\phi:G\to G$ is a <group isomorphism> if it is a <bijection> and a <group homomorphism>, meaning
$$
\phi(xy)=\phi(x)\phi(y)\qquad(x,y\in G).
$$
Such a map from a <group> to itself is an <group automorphism>. Its inverse is also a <group homomorphism>: if $u=\phi(x)$ and $v=\phi(y)$, then $\phi^{-1}(uv)=xy=\phi^{-1}(u)\phi^{-1}(v)$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Write $C_p=\langle g\rangle$. A <group homomorphism> from this <cyclic group> is determined by the image of $g$, because $\phi(g^j)=\phi(g)^j$. Each choice $\phi(g)=g^a$, with $a=0,\ldots,p-1$, gives a well-defined <group homomorphism>. It is an <group automorphism> exactly when $g^a$ generates $C_p$.

For a <prime number> $p$, every $a=1,\ldots,p-1$ is <coprime> to $p$, so the powers of $g^a$ run through all of $C_p$. The choice $a=0$ gives the trivial <group homomorphism> and fails to be bijective. Consequently
$$
\boxed{|\operatorname{Aut}(C_p)|=p-1.}
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

For $h\in H$, define $\pi(h)(n)=hnh^{-1}$. Since $N$ is a <normal subgroup>, this sends $N$ into itself. It preserves multiplication, and its inverse is conjugation by $h^{-1}$, so it is an <group automorphism> of $N$. Moreover
$$
\pi(h_1h_2)(n)=h_1h_2n h_2^{-1}h_1^{-1}
=\bigl(\pi(h_1)\circ\pi(h_2)\bigr)(n).
$$
Thus $\pi:H\to\operatorname{Aut}(N)$ is a <group homomorphism>, induced by the <group action> of <conjugation>.

If $\pi$ is trivial, every element of $H$ commutes with every element of $N$. Hence
$$
F:N\times H\longrightarrow G,\qquad F(n,h)=nh
$$
is a <group homomorphism>, since $(n_1h_1)(n_2h_2)=n_1n_2h_1h_2$. Its <image of a group homomorphism> is a <subgroup> containing both $N$ and $H$, and every product $nh$ lies in $\langle N,H\rangle$, so its image is exactly $\langle N,H\rangle$. If $nh=e$, then $n=h^{-1}\in N\cap H=\{e\}$, so $n=h=e$. Thus $F$ is injective and gives
$$
\boxed{\langle N,H\rangle\cong N\times H.}
$$

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}
{c}

The <stabilizer subgroup> of $H$ for this <conjugation> <group action> is its <normalizer>
$$
\operatorname{Stab}(H)=N_G(H)=\{g\in G:gHg^{-1}=H\}.
$$
If it is all of $G$, the equality $gHg^{-1}=H$ holds for every $g\in G$, which is precisely the definition of a <normal subgroup>.

If instead $\operatorname{Stab}(H)=H$, the <orbit-stabilizer theorem> says that the <group orbit> of $H$ has size $[G:H]$. Every conjugate $gHg^{-1}$ is isomorphic to $H$, with <group isomorphism> $h\mapsto ghg^{-1}$, so this <group orbit> is a <subset> of $\Omega$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{|\Omega|\geq[G:H].}
$$
The <group action> need not be transitive on all of $\Omega$; this one <group orbit> supplies the lower bound.

