= Solution
{parent=i}
{c}

By <Lagrange's theorem>, every nonidentity <group element> has order $5$, $7$, or $35$. If some element has order $35$, its seventh and fifth powers have orders $5$ and $7$, respectively, and we are done.

Otherwise every nonidentity element has order $5$ or $7$. Distinct <subgroups> of a given <prime number> order intersect only in the identity: a shared nonidentity element generates both. If there were no element of order $5$, the $34$ nonidentity elements would therefore partition into sets of six, the nonidentity elements of the <cyclic subgroups> of order seven. This would force $6\mid34$, a contradiction. If there were no element of order $7$, the analogous partition into sets of four would force $4\mid34$, again a contradiction. Thus **elements of both orders exist**.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Let $P$ and $Q$ be <subgroups> of orders five and seven, which exist by <6/c/i>. Their <normalizers> contain $P$ and $Q$, respectively. By <Lagrange's theorem>, $N_G(P)$ has order $5$ or $35$, and $N_G(Q)$ has order $7$ or $35$. If either has order $35$, its <subgroup> is normal by <6/b/iv>.

Suppose neither is normal. Then <6/b/iv> gives at least seven distinct <subgroups> of order five and at least five distinct <subgroups> of order seven. Distinct <subgroups> of the same prime order intersect only in the identity, and a <subgroup> of order five and one of order seven also intersect only in the identity by <Lagrange's theorem>. Thus $G$ would have at least
$$
7(5-1)+5(7-1)=58
$$
nonidentity elements, contradicting $|G|-1=34$. Hence **at least one of $P,Q$ is a normal subgroup**.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

Choose a <normal subgroup> $N$ of prime order $p\in\{5,7\}$ as in <6/c/ii>, and choose a <subgroup> $H$ of the other prime order $q$. Their intersection is trivial by <Lagrange's theorem>. Both are <cyclic groups>, and <6/b/ii> gives $|\operatorname{Aut}(N)|=p-1$.

The <image of a group homomorphism> $\pi:H\to\operatorname{Aut}(N)$ from <6/b/iii> has order dividing $q$, because its <kernel of a group homomorphism> partitions $H$ into equally sized <cosets>. Its order also divides $p-1$ by <Lagrange's theorem>. But
$$
\gcd(7,4)=\gcd(5,6)=1,
$$
so $\pi$ is trivial in either case. By <6/b/iii>, $\langle N,H\rangle\cong C_p\times C_q$ has order $pq=35$ and therefore equals $G$.

If $n$ and $h$ generate $N$ and $H$, the element $(n,h)$ has order $\operatorname{lcm}(p,q)=pq=35$, since its $k$th power is the identity exactly when both $p$ and $q$ divide $k$. Thus this <direct product of groups> is cyclic, and
$$
\boxed{G\cong C_{35}.}
$$

