For $X\in\operatorname{SO}_3(\mathbb R)$, the defining identity $X^TX=I$ gives
$$
\|Xv\|^2=v^TX^TXv=v^Tv=\|v\|^2.
$$
Thus an <orthogonal transformation> preserves the <Euclidean norm> and sends the <unit sphere> to itself. Restricting the given <group action> to this invariant set preserves both action axioms.

If $XN=N$, the first column of $X$ is $N=e_1$. The other columns are <unit vectors> perpendicular to it, so the first row is also $(1,0,0)$. Hence $X=\operatorname{diag}(1,B)$ with $B^TB=I_2$ and $\det B=1$. Conversely, every such <matrix> lies in $G$ and fixes $N$. Since every element of the <special orthogonal group in two dimensions> has the planar <rotation matrix> form, the <stabilizer subgroup> is
$$
\boxed{H=\left\{R_\theta=
\begin{pmatrix}
1&0&0\\
0&\cos\theta&-\sin\theta\\
0&\sin\theta&\cos\theta
\end{pmatrix}:\theta\in\mathbb R\right\}\cong\operatorname{SO}_2(\mathbb R).}
$$
Geometrically these are exactly the <rotations in three dimensions> about the first coordinate axis.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}
{c}

Let $v\in S$. Choose a <unit vector> $u$ perpendicular to $v$, and put $w=v\times u$. The <cross product> identities show that $(v,u,w)$ is an <orthonormal basis> with positive orientation: $u\times(v\times u)=v$, so
$$
\det\begin{pmatrix}v&u&w\end{pmatrix}
=v\cdot(u\times w)=v\cdot v=1.
$$
Thus the <matrix> $A_v$ with these columns belongs to the <special orthogonal group> $G$ and satisfies $A_vN=v$. For any $v,z\in S$, the element $A_zA_v^{-1}$ sends $v$ to $z$. Therefore the <group action> on the <unit sphere> is **transitive**.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}
{c}

For any integer $n\geq1$, let $R=R_{2\pi/n}$ in the <stabilizer subgroup> from <7/b/i>. Since $R_\theta R_\phi=R_{\theta+\phi}$ and $R_\theta=I$ exactly when $\theta\in2\pi\mathbb Z$, this <rotation matrix> has order exactly $n$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\langle R_{2\pi/n}\rangle\cong C_n}
$$
is a <subgroup> of $G$ of order $n$, including the trivial case $n=1$.

For infinitely many non-Abelian examples, let $n\geq3$, retain $R=R_{2\pi/n}$, and take the half-turn
$$
J=\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,-1)\in G.
$$
Direct multiplication gives $J^2=I$ and $JRJ=R^{-1}$. Every word in $R,J$ therefore reduces to $R^k$ or $R^kJ$, $0\leq k<n$. The $R^k$ are distinct, as are the $R^kJ$, and no element from the first family equals one from the second: they send $N$ to $N$ and $-N$, respectively. Hence $\langle R,J\rangle$ has exactly $2n$ elements and is a <dihedral group>.

It is non-Abelian because $R\ne R^{-1}$ for $n\geq3$, so $JR\ne RJ$. Different values of $n$ give different <group orders>, and therefore different <subgroups>. Thus **$G$ has infinitely many non-Abelian subgroups**, already among its finite <subgroups>.
